  "generated_text": "Let's carefully review the solution step by step to ensure there are no errors.\n\nWe start with the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\). We make the substitution \\( w = z^2 \\), transforming the equation to:\n\\[\nw^2 + w + 1 = 0.\n\\]\n\nThe roots of the quadratic equation \\( w^2 + w + 1 = 0 \\) are found using the quadratic formula:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThese roots are:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad w = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRecalling that \\( w = z^2 \\), we need to find the values of \\( z \\) such that:\n\\[\nz^2 = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad z^2 = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe roots of these equations are the non-real cube roots of unity, \\( \\omega \\) and \\( \\omega^2 \\), where:\n\\[\n\\omega = e^{2\\pi i / 3} = -\\frac{1}{2} + i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\omega^2 = e^{-2\\pi i / 3} = -\\frac{1}{2} - i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe cube roots of unity are \\( 1, \\omega, \\omega^2 \\). The roots \\( \\omega \\) and \\( \\omega^2 \\) are indeed 6th roots of unity because:\n\\[\n\\omega^3 = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (\\omega^2)^3 = 1.\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n \\)-th roots of unity is \\( n = 6 \\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
